              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_3_dimensions"} In 3 dimensions
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general_and_higher_dimensions"} General and higher dimensions
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_11d"} In 11d
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#boundary_theories"} Boundary theories
          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#general General
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"}> Cartan connection
            ) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            3 happens to be equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections
            with values in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+Lie+algebra"} Poincare Lie algebra
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+connection"} spin connection
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vielbein"} vielbein
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+quantum+gravity"} 3d quantum gravity
          %p
            Generally, for every higher degree (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            \-ary)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            on the Poincaré Lie algebra there is the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            in dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n-1
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            linear in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            as a summand, but generally contain higher degree curvature invariants. A theory of
            %em Chern-Simons gravity
            is a field theory governed by such an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
          %p
            While there is no experimental evidence for such higher curvature terms in the theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/In%C3%B6n%C3%BC-Wigner+contraction"} Inönü-Wigner contraction
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS+Lie+algebra"} AdS Lie algebra
            to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+Lie+algebra"} Poincare Lie algebra
            = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi so
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} so(n,2)
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+gravity"} anti de Sitter gravity
          %p
            In view of this, the fact that Chern-Simons action functionals are singled out by their nice formal properties has led to various speculations that possibly the fundamental theory of gravity is secretly a theory of Chern-Simons gravity after all (see the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "References"} References
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
            the study of Chern-Simons gravity is natural and interesting.
          %p
            %strong Warning
            In parts of the literature the term
            %em Chern-Simons gravity
            is used for the modification of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            in 4-dimensions obtained by adding to the standard action the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontryagin+class"} Pontryagin class
            term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi R
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_X \langle R \wedge R\rangle
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+curvature"} Riemann curvature
          %h3#ProblemsInTheNonPerturbativeRegime Problems in the non-perturbative regime
          %p
            The above discussion of the relation between 3-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert gravity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            certainly makes sense in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbatively
            the relation between the two theories is at best much more subtle and apparently in fact not existent.
          %p
            Citing from (
            %a{:href => "#Witten07"}> Witten 07, pages 4,5,6
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+field+theory"}> nonperturbatively
              , the relation between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+quantum+gravity"} three-dimensional gravity
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons gauge theory
              is unclear. For one thing, in Chern-Simons theory, nonperturbatively the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vierbein"} vierbein
              may cease to be invertible. For example, there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} classical solution
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %mi ω
                      %mo =
                      %mi e
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \omega = e = 0
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Witten88"} Witten 88
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nathan+Seiberg"} N. Seiberg
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity in two spacetime dimensions
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
              over
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"} Riemann surfaces
              that leads to a sensible theory is derived assuming that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} metric
              should be non-degenerate. There are other possible problems in the nonperturbative relation between threedimensional gravity and Chern-Simons theory. The equivalence between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphisms"} diffeomorphisms
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
              is limited to diffeomorphisms that are continuously connected to the identity. However, in gravity, we believe that more general diffeomorphisms (such as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+transformations"} modular transformations
              in
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} quantum Hilbert space
              by quantizing the Chern-Simons gauge fields on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liouville+theory"} Liouville theory
              [8-11].) In
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT"} gauge/gravity relation
              was suggested, it was discovered by Bañados, Teitelboim, and Zanelli [12] that in three-dimensional gravity with negative cosmological constant, there are black hole solutions. The existence of these objects, generally called
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BTZ+black+holes"} BTZ black holes
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bekenstein-Hawking+entropy"} Bekenstein-Hawking entropy
          %p
            See also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence"} AdS3-CFT2 and CS-WZW correspondence
            for (pointers to) discussion of how some variant of Chern-Simons theory appears as one “sector” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT"} AdS/CFT
            in 3 dimensions/2-dimensions (and indeed not as the quantum gravity sector).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"}> gravity
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+formulation+of+gravity"} first-order formulation of gravity
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+quantum+gravity"} 3d quantum gravity
          %h2#References References
          %h3#in_3_dimensions In 3 dimensions
          %p
            The original articles that considered 3-dimensional gravity as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+gravity"}> anti de Sitter gravity
            ) are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A. Achúcarro and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Townsend"} Paul Townsend
                %em A Chern-Simons Action for Three-Dimensional anti-De Sitter Supergravity Theories
                , Phys. Lett. B180 (1986) 89.
            %li#Witten88
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                %em (2+1)-Dimensional Gravity as an Exactly Soluble System
                Nucl. Phys. B311 (1988) 46. (
                %a{:href => "http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1988NuPhB.311...46W"}> web
                )
          %p Further developments are in
          %ul
            %li#Witten07
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              %em Three-Dimensional Gravity Revisited
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0706.3359"}> arXiv:0706.3359
              )
          %p A review of some aspects is in
          %ul
            %li
              Bastian Wemmenhove,
              %em Quantisation of 2+1 dimensional Gravity as a Chern-Simons theory
              thesis (2002) (
              %a{:href => "http://staff.science.uva.nl/~bais/scripties/wemmenhoven.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Boundaries in 3d Chern-Simons gravity and relation to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            are discussed for instance in
          %ul
            %li
              Giovanni Arcioni,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthias+Blau"} Matthias Blau
              %em On the boundary dynamics of Chern-Simons gravity
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0210089"}> arXiv:0210089
              )
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+quantum+gravity"} 3d quantum gravity
          %h3#general_and_higher_dimensions General and higher dimensions
          %p An introduction and survey is in
          %ul
            %li#Zanelli05
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jorge+Zanelli"}> Jorge Zanelli
              ,
              %em Lecture notes on Chern-Simons (super-)gravities
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0502193"}> arXiv:hep-th/0502193
              )
          %p More along these lines is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jorge+Zanelli"}> Jorge Zanelli
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Chern-Simons Forms in Gravitation Theories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1208.3353"} arXiv:1208.3353
          %p Original articles include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ali+Chamseddine"}> Ali Chamseddine
                ,
                %em Topological gravity and supergravity in various dimensions
                Nuclear Physics B346 (1990) 213—234 (
                %a{:href => "http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1990NuPhB.346..213C"}> web
                )
            %li
              %p
                Máximo Bañados,
                %em Higher dimensional Chern-Simons theories and black holes
                (
                %a{:href => "http://worldscibooks.com/etextbook/4388/4388_chap01.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Máximo Bañados, Ricardo Troncoso, Jorge Zanelli,
                %em Higher dimensional Chern-Simons supergravity
                Phys. Rev. D 54, 2605–2611 (1996)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jorge+Zanelli"}> Jorge Zanelli
                ,
                %em Chern–Simons forms and transgression actions or the universe as a subsystem
                Journal of Physics: Conference Series Volume 68 Volume 68
            %li
              %p
                Hitoshi Nishino and Subhash Rajpoot,
                %em Supersymmetric Lorentz Chern-Simons terms coupled to supergravity
                Phys. Rev. D 81, 085029 (2010)
          %h3#in_11d In 11d
          %p
            A speculation that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            is naturally to be understood as a contraction limit of a Chern-Simons supergravity theory was put forward in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Petr+Ho%C5%99ava"}> Petr Hořava
              ,
              %em M-Theory as a Holographic Field Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9712130"}> arXiv:hep-th/9712130
              )
          %p with further developments in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Horatiu+Nastase"}> Horatiu Nastase
              ,
              %em
                Towards a Chern-Simons M theory of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi OSp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 32
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi OSp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 32
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} OSp(1|32)\times OSp(1|32)
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0306269"}> arXiv:hep-th/0306269
              )
          %p Other approaches to 11d Chern-Simons supergravity include
          %ul
            %li
              Fernando Izaurieta, Eduardo Rodríguez,
              %em On eleven-dimensional Supergravity and Chern-Simons theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1103.2182"}> arXiv:1103.2182
              )
          %p
            There is an 6-ary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 12
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 12 = 2 \cdot 6
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+super+Lie+algebra"} M-theory super Lie algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+element"} Chern-Simons element
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            for an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
            yields an 11-dimensional supersymmetric field theory different from but maybe related to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Mokhtar Hassaine, Ricardo Troncoso,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jorge+Zanelli"} Jorge Zanelli
                %em Poincaré invariant gravity with local supersymmetry as a gauge theory for the M-algebra
                , Phys. Lett. B596 (2004) 132-137 (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0306258"}> arXiv:hep-th/0306258
                )
            %li
              %p
                Mokhtar Hassaine, Ricardo Troncoso,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jorge+Zanelli"} Jorge Zanelli
                %em 11D Supergravity as a gauge theory for the M-algebra
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0503220"}> arXiv:hep-th/0503220
                )
          %p
            A discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+elements"} Chern-Simons elements
            in higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            and their relation not quite to Chern-Simons forms but to their curvature-first-order-analog – the
            %em cosmo-cocycle condition
            – is at
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%27Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity"} D'Auria-Fre formulation of supergravity
              \.
          %h3#boundary_theories Boundary theories
          %p
            Boundary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+WZW+models"} higher dimensional WZW models
            for nonabelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            are discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                J. Gegenberg , G. Kunstatter,
                %em Boundary Dynamics of Higher Dimensional Chern-Simons Gravity
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0010020"}> arXiv:hep-th/0010020
                )
            %li
              %p
                J. Gegenberg , G. Kunstatter,
                %em Boundary Dynamics of Higher Dimensional AdS Spacetime
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9905228"}> arXiv:http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9905228
                )
